{
  "teamId": 133,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Oakland Athletics arrived with genuine optimism, and April validated it in a hurry. Matt Olson's early three-run blast set the tone for a rotation-and-power formula that carried the A's to a 16-11 start, one of the best records in the American League. The lineup showed pop, the pitching was sharp, and the Coliseum buzzed with playoff expectations. But the month wasn't without turbulence — a catastrophic throwing error by Ramón Laureano erased a critical game against Minnesota, and Brett Phillips went deep off Frankie Montas to spoil another — previewing the self-inflicted wounds that would haunt Oakland all season.\n\nMay brought the first real wobble. Jake Diekman surrendered back-to-back late-game home runs on consecutive nights, and the bullpen began showing cracks that would widen over time. Still, the team scratched out a 15-14 record, staying relevant. June was the high-water mark: a 17-9 month fueled by Tony Kemp's clutch three-run shot and a lineup that was clicking on all cylinders. By late June the A's sat comfortably above .500 and appeared bound for a second consecutive playoff berth. Then the calendar flipped to July, and the slide began in earnest.\n\nJuly and August were a season in miniature — thrilling highs and gutting lows arriving in rapid succession. Jed Lowrie's own go-ahead homer crushed a critical home game in mid-July in one of the season's most deflating moments. The team stumbled to an 11-13 July mark, and August offered only modest repair. Lou Trivino, for all his late-season struggles, was also the source of unexpected heroism: opponents hit walkoff shots against him, but he also stranded runs in crucial spots. A.J. Puk and the bullpen occasionally steadied things, and the offense — led by Olson, Canha, and a rotating cast — kept Oakland within striking distance of a Wild Card spot.\n\nSeptember, however, was where Oakland's hopes unraveled. A 12-15 record in the final full month told the story plainly. Marcus Semien, now in Toronto, crushed a Sergio Romo pitch for a three-run homer that felt like a gut punch delivered by an old friend. Grand slams, late-game singles, and a Starling Marte double in the penultimate week of the season each chipped away at the margin. The A's finished 86-76 — a winning record, 743 runs scored, a solid ERA — and missed the playoffs entirely. It was a season of genuine talent and genuine heartbreak, a team that played well enough to win and found just enough ways to lose.",
